  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following compounds is likely to show both Frenkel and Schottky defects in its crystalline form?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgBr</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CsCl</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KBr</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ZnS</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgBr</mi></math> can exhibit both Schottky and Frenkel defect.</p><p>Schottky&nbsp;defect&nbsp;in&nbsp;<em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgBr</mi></math></em>&nbsp;is exhibited due to the precipitation of both cations and anions. In&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgBr</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;ion is small in size and when removed&nbsp;from the lattice point, they can occupy the interstitial sites and, hence,&nbsp;shows&nbsp;both Frenkel and Schottky&nbsp;defects.</p>"
  }
}